From 0db1b4fd15cade3fb9c200c793ec5dab2d5faeb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 22:59:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gallium/pvr: sRGB textures and render targets, and
 multi-dword colour outputs

The TPU and PBE both have gamma modes; wiring them up gives ES2's GL_EXT_sRGB (and is the format
foundation ES3 needs for SRGB8_ALPHA8):

- Sampling: sRGB images get TEXSTATE_IMAGE_WORD0's gamma bit (the reference sets it for sRGB
  formats). The non-obvious half was TEXSTATE_IMAGE_WORD1's alpha_msb: without it the TPU treats the
  first memory channel as alpha and leaves it un-decoded, so a sampled sRGB texel came back with red
  still sRGB-encoded while green/blue were linear (40% error on red only). Setting alpha_msb where the
  format's fourth channel is W (the reference's vk_format_is_alpha_on_msb rule) fixes the decode.
- Rendering: colour targets get PBESTATE_REG_WORD0's gamma bit, so the PBE encodes the linear source
  values on write; the PBE source format for an sRGB bitmap is 16-bit float per channel (the same
  F16 conversion pco applies to shader outputs), so the background clear packs the clear colour as
  linear half floats instead of memory-order bytes.
- Multi-dword colour outputs: an sRGB (16F source) attachment occupies two pixel-output dwords, so
  the fragment variant's output ranges, the background clear's per-dword stores, the EOT's PBE
  source_pos and the ISP pixel-output width are all built from a shared "PBE output dwords" count
  instead of assuming one dword per attachment. This is also the plumbing 16F/32F colour targets need.
- A deferred clear now keeps the float clear colour on the resource, not only the packed dword: the
  packed value is right for one format while the deferred render may need the colour in another
  encoding (this is what made a deferred sRGB clear come out black).
- Format support: R8G8B8A8_SRGB/B8G8R8A8_SRGB/A8B8G8R8_SRGB as sampler views and render targets;
  GL_EXT_sRGB is advertised on ES2 now.

Verified against zink with open-perf/srgb_test.c: uploaded sRGB texel bytes (128,64,32) decode to
(55,13,4) - identical; an sRGB target cleared to linear 0.5 samples back 128 and a 0.25 draw samples
back 64 - identical. Full gate green: 49 gltri modes (all native glGetError 0x0), 28 mip modes,
rttest PASS (bad=0/262144), rt8test unchanged, 2/4-attachment and mixed-format MRT unchanged,
instancing unchanged, 0 MMU faults.
